Formant-Grid Electro

Ingredients: YAKUT KHOMUS + DETROIT ELECTRO / MINIMAL SYNTH + PREPARED CLAVICHORD + DRY ROTOTOMS / WOODBLOCK + TENOR SAXOPHONE + SHORT MONO BASS

Creative Catalyst: The genre began during a winter residency in an abandoned radio-phonetics institute outside Yakutsk, where khomus player Sardaana Petrovna was demonstrating how one plucked metal tongue could produce radically different perceived pitches without changing its fundamental vibration. By reshaping the mouth cavity, tongue position, throat opening, and breath pressure, she could move bright harmonic bands through the same underlying drone. Berlin electro producer Leon Hartmann realized that the physical principle resembled a filter sweep, but with one crucial difference: the source oscillator was acoustic and almost unchanged while the human vocal tract performed the spectral motion. Instead of sampling the khomus and running it through an electronic filter, he froze the synth line into square, discrete notes and built the arrangement around Sardaana’s continuous formant movement. Prepared clavichord, dry rototoms, woodblock, tenor saxophone, and short mono bass completed a style where the mouth acts like a living resonant filter against rigid machine geometry.

Formant-Grid Electro is a dry acoustic-electronic dance form built from spectral motion without melodic motion. Detroit electro and minimal synth supply the chassis: 112–124 BPM, compact machine kick, dry snare, syncopated handclap, short sixteenth-note hats, rigid mono-bass cells, small interval sequences, and deliberately exposed negative space. The Yakut khomus supplies the opposite logic through one vibrating metal tongue, pulse articulation, overtone selection, jaw resonance, tongue shaping, throat opening, inhaled and exhaled pressure, and hand damping.

The khomus is recorded with a miniature condenser near the frame but outside direct breath turbulence, one ribbon microphone roughly half a meter in front of the performer, and one restrained room microphone. The close channel captures the metal tongue attack and transient pulse. The ribbon captures the changing vocal-cavity resonance and body. The room channel is automated upward only during wide open-mouth states so the acoustic spectral expansion remains spatially legible.

Players favor repeated tonic pulses, alternating strong and weak plucks, controlled jaw opening, tongue-forward and tongue-back resonance, throat-open low formants, narrow vowel-like sweeps, breath-assisted swells, muted stops, and occasional asymmetrical hand accents. Conventional melodic imitation is avoided. The defining gesture is a nearly fixed fundamental whose apparent tonal center shifts because the resonant cavity above it keeps changing.

The central compositional device is the Formant Grid. Every major khomus phrase passes through four mouth states: NARROW, FRONT, HOLLOW, and OPEN. NARROW uses a small cavity and produces a focused upper harmonic. FRONT pushes the tongue forward and emphasizes brighter partials. HOLLOW opens the throat while keeping the lips restrained, strengthening lower resonances. OPEN expands the full mouth cavity and produces the broadest overtone field. The metal tongue continues pulsing through all four states.

Electronic instruments respond to those spectral states rather than tracking pitch. NARROW can trigger the driest kick-and-hat pattern. FRONT permits clipped synth syncopations. HOLLOW often removes upper percussion and lets the mono bass become more active. OPEN can reduce the entire machine arrangement to kick and one chord so the wider acoustic resonance becomes obvious.

A second device, Fixed Note / Moving Vowel, keeps the synth and bass on one repeated pitch or two-note cell while the khomus changes apparent color through mouth shape. This makes acoustic spectral movement more important than chord progression.

A third device, Cavity Lock, freezes the player in one mouth position for several bars while the electronic sequence changes underneath. The effect reverses the usual hierarchy: the machine becomes mobile and the body becomes static.

A fourth device, Breath Offset, adds soft inhaled or exhaled airflow immediately before or after selected plucks. The breath is not used as ambience; it creates a secondary pulse around the metal attack and can displace the perceived groove without altering the sequencer clock.

Drums remain dry and mechanical. Kick is compact, often alternating between straight quarter-note support and broken electro placement. Snare is short and un-reverberant. Handclaps appear sparingly. Closed hats use simple sixteenth patterns with abrupt omissions. Rototoms provide dry pitched punctuation at section boundaries. Woodblock occupies the sharp transient range when the khomus itself becomes spectrally broad.

The short mono bass uses root, fifth, octave, flat seventh, and occasional semitone approach notes. It never follows the changing khomus formants as if they were melody. Its function is to establish a stable low reference against which the mouth-driven overtone motion can be perceived.

Prepared clavichord supplies brittle harmonic checkpoints. Selected strings are muted with removable felt or paper, and tangents are recorded close enough to preserve the mechanical contact. Parts use open fifths, minor sevenths, suspended seconds, repeated single notes, and short two-note cadences. The clavichord is most active when the khomus is held in Cavity Lock and simplest during broad OPEN states.

Tenor saxophone supplies a warm aerodynamic countervoice through subtone pedals, short fourths, key clicks, tongue stops, breath-only attacks, and restrained multiphonic edges. Saxophone entries are brief and generally appear when the mono bass drops out, giving the low-mid register a human air column without copying the khomus.

Vocals are optional and sparse. Sakha, German, Russian, or English fragments may reference vowel charts, radio tests, mouth diagrams, transmitter logs, cavity numbers, or instructions such as “hold the vowel,” “same tongue,” “open the room,” and “do not change the note.” Delivery should be spoken, whispered, or rhythmically clipped.

Effects remain restrained. Khomus stays mostly dry with a short room and very small plate used only in OPEN states. Clavichord receives a tiny chamber. Tenor saxophone gets a dark room. Drum bus compression is firm and fast. Electronic filters are used conservatively because the main spectral motion belongs to the human mouth. No vocoder, talkbox, auto-wah khomus, cinematic Siberian drones, trance pads, long 808 glides, or synthetic jaw-filter imitation is allowed.

The defining tension is spectral freedom against pitch rigidity. The metal tongue barely changes its fundamental while the performer’s mouth makes the sound appear to travel through several invisible rooms.

Genre DNA

- Tempo & Pulse: 112–124 BPM, mostly 4/4 Detroit electro / minimal-synth architecture with compact kick, dry snare, sparse clap, restrained sixteenth hats, rigid mono-bass cells, and khomus pulses that can sit on or between machine subdivisions.
- Harmonic Palette: Dorian, Aeolian, open fifths, suspended seconds, minor sevenths, single-note pedals, semitone approaches, and long static harmonic zones designed to expose overtone movement.
- Production Signatures:
    1. Formant Grid — khomus phrases move through NARROW → FRONT → HOLLOW → OPEN mouth-resonance states.
    2. Fixed Note / Moving Vowel — electronic pitch remains nearly static while acoustic formants shift continuously.
    3. Cavity Lock — the player freezes one mouth shape while the machine arrangement changes underneath.
    4. Breath Offset — inhaled or exhaled airflow is positioned around selected metal-tongue attacks as a secondary rhythmic event.
    5. Open-State Clearance — hats, claps, and high synth material thin when the khomus reaches its broadest resonance.
    6. No-Electronic-Formant-Doubling Rule — no vocoder, talkbox, auto-wah, or resonant filter may mimic the principal khomus spectral path.
- Dynamics: Intensity grows through stronger pluck amplitude, faster mouth-state changes, denser electro syncopation, firmer rototom accents, brighter clavichord attacks, and wider contrast between HOLLOW and OPEN. Climaxes stay skeletal and high-contrast rather than layered.

Scene & Origin Lore

Formant-Grid Electro formed inside a retired radio-phonetics and speech-transmission institute on the outskirts of Yakutsk. The building retained vowel charts, reel-to-reel calibration tapes, acoustic booths, waveform printouts, analog oscilloscopes, steel microphone stands, patch bays, snow-frosted windows, and rows of labeled resonance tubes once used for speech experiments.

Khomus player Sardaana Petrovna had been using one of the dry voice booths to practice overtone isolation because the room made small changes in mouth shape unusually obvious. She was less interested in playing many notes than in making one vibrating tongue produce several perceived spectral identities.

German producer Leon Hartmann arrived with a compact drum machine, step sequencer, and mono synth. His first attempt processed Sardaana through an envelope filter. The result immediately destroyed the point of the instrument. He removed the processor, locked the synth to one note, and let the human vocal tract supply all spectral movement.

Prepared-clavichord player Maren Vogt joined after developing short mechanical chord shells that could function as fixed reference points. Percussionist Nyurgun Afanasyev reduced his acoustic setup to two rototoms and one woodblock. Tenor-saxophonist Timur Lebedev completed the ensemble with low breath, key noise, and subtone phrases for bass-free sections.

Their first performances were called mouth maps. A projection behind the group showed four abstract cross-sections labeled NARROW, FRONT, HOLLOW, and OPEN. The diagrams were not synchronized to sensors and controlled nothing. They existed only as a listening guide.

The visual language developed around oscilloscope green, winter grey, microphone silver, faded chart cream, cold fluorescent white, dark khomus steel, graphite black, and muted signal red. The unofficial rule is that if an audience member mistakes the main spectral sweep for an electronic filter, the machine arrangement is too busy.

